            H3("DevOps and Kanban"),
            "DevOps and Kanban reinforce each other. Kanban makes the whole value stream visible, including operational work such as incidents and maintenance that is often invisible on development boards; DORA lists [visual management](/project-management/kanban/visual-management/), visibility of work in the value stream and WIP limits among its capabilities. Kanban's flow metrics then describe the work end to end, while DORA's metrics focus on the stretch from commit to production.",
            "The two sets of measures differ in scope. [Lead time](/project-management/kanban/cycle-time-vs-lead-time/) in Kanban usually starts when a request is accepted; DORA's change lead time starts only when code is committed. A team can have a change lead time of a few hours and still keep customers waiting weeks, because most of the delay happens before anyone writes code. Track both. Teams running a mix of planned work and interruptions will find practical policies, including reserved capacity and classes of service, in our guide to [Agile IT operations](/project-management/agile/agile-operations-it/).",
            H3("DevSecOps and platform engineering"),
            "**DevSecOps** moves security into the same flow: dependency scanning, static analysis and policy checks run in the pipeline on every change, and security specialists help teams build safe defaults instead of inspecting finished releases. DORA calls this capability pervasive security.",
